---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/drip-alternative';
const title = "Drip alternatives: preserve the journey before replacing the sender";
const desc = "Choose between repairing Drip workflows, adding an SMS sender and replacing the lifecycle platform without restarting queued customers.";
const faqs = [{"question": "Should a working Drip email program be replaced to add SMS?", "answer": "Not automatically. Check current Drip SMS eligibility and repair the existing workflow first. A separate sender is worth evaluating when a required market, phone-only signup or conversation path is outside your account\u2019s scope."}, {"question": "Do exported people recreate Drip workflows?", "answer": "No. Rebuild branch conditions, tag meanings, event fields, templates and queue position explicitly. Importing a person must not restart a welcome offer or replay an expired cart reminder."}, {"question": "What is the cutover release condition?", "answer": "Release a bounded journey only after its operator can demonstrate permission checks, purchase exits, replay protection and ownership of waiting people. Keep a pause procedure and an event ledger for rollback."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p>{desc}</p>
    <h2>Keep the visual workflow when it is still useful</h2>
    <p>Drip’s visual email journeys can retain valuable product education, conditional branches and maintained audience logic. Repair an ambiguous tag, stale offer or missing exit before paying to reconstruct the entire program. For a hypothetical high-value audio store, keep comparison emails and reserve launch access for an explicitly eligible audience; no merchant result or conversion uplift is claimed here. The proposed exercises below are acceptance criteria, not tests we have performed.</p>
    <h2>Check current SMS access before choosing a replacement</h2>
    <p>Checked November 25, 2026: <a href="https://www.drip.com/sms">Drip’s SMS page</a> describes phased access for existing US-based paid customers with Support All People enabled. Numbers require an email; phone-only subscribers are unsupported. Launch sending is one-way toll-free, without keyword-to-join, short codes or two-way replies. These account constraints can justify another SMS path while retaining email. Confirm actual activation rather than calling Drip email-only or assuming access is universal.</p>
    <h2>Choose the boundary of the change</h2>
    <p>Retaining Drip and adding a sender preserves email investment but creates a second permission, event and billing boundary. A full lifecycle replacement can consolidate day-to-day ownership if one team can rebuild the logic. Investigate <a href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> for a separate SMS evaluation, or <a href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> and <a href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> for a broader platform evaluation. These are candidates to demonstrate your requirements, not ranked winners. Require a sample branch and purchase exit in the destination before selecting it.</p>
    <h2>Reconstruct branches, tags and events</h2>
    <p>Inventory each active workflow’s entry trigger, re-entry rule, tag changes, delays, yes/no conditions and exits. Separate a durable preference tag from a temporary campaign marker. Record the exact Shopify event name, order/cart ID, currency, variant and timestamp fields used by each decision. A destination field with a similar name is not proof of equal meaning. Assign a technical owner to map events and a campaign owner to approve branch behavior; retain screenshots and configuration exports for comparison.</p>
    <h2>Translate templates and waiting people separately</h2>
    <p>Export the editable copy, fallback values, links, discount eligibility and expiry of each email. Preview missing names and unavailable products in the new template rather than copying syntax blindly. Snapshot people waiting at every delay with their original entry time, completed steps and next eligible action. Choose whether each cohort finishes in Drip, resumes at an equivalent step, or is deliberately cancelled. Never bulk import that snapshot into a live entry trigger. Keep one queue owner per cohort and an auditable decision for anyone excluded.</p>
    <h2>Proposed reconstruction acceptance fixture</h2>
    <p>Create separate fixtures for a new subscriber, a returning buyer, a changed email, an email-only person and a separately SMS-permitted person. Send a cart event, duplicate it, delay a purchase event and then replay the original cart event. Expected behavior: one relevant journey, no second welcome incentive and no cart reminder after the corresponding purchase has been processed. Log event arrival and dispatch times to expose ingestion lag. Rehearse a tag removed during a delay and a customer qualifying for two branches; the campaign owner must sign off on the selected path.</p>
    <h2>Preserve the promise and calendar</h2>
    <p>Move source, timestamp and disclosure evidence with each channel permission, plus withdrawals and suppression reasons. Missing evidence should hold the affected channel out of migration. Preserve gift versus self-purchase preferences, subscription state and collector waitlists when they have a documented meaning. For a limited release, validate inventory before access messaging and expire the offer when stock is gone. Set recipient and market scheduling rules, combined contact limits and support exclusions from your requirements; a universal monthly cap or legal time window is not supplied here.</p>
    <h2>Price the retained and rebuilt work</h2>
    <p>Request current quotes at ordinary and peak volume, including email contacts, SMS segments, destination/carrier charges and overlap subscriptions. Add template rebuilding, integration maintenance and monitored support time. A hypothetical order priced at $100 with a $10 discount and fixed $45 goods cost leaves $45 before fulfillment, refunds and messaging, not a percentage margin held constant after discount. Count the discount once through net sales. Compare same-period contribution per assigned eligible customer with a holdout, subtracting goods, fulfillment, messaging and allocated platform/support cost; attributed revenue alone is not incremental benefit.</p>
    <h2>Cut over one journey with a replay ledger</h2>
    <p>Start with a bounded cohort whose expected next action is known. Freeze its old entry path before enabling the new one; leave unrelated working journeys alone. Reconcile cohort counts, exclusions, completed orders and pending sends across both systems. Keep essential order/service communication with an explicitly named owner, outside marketing holdouts. Give one operator the authority to stop dispatch if duplicate entries, permission mismatches or missing exits exceed the acceptance limits agreed before launch.</p>
    <h2>Rollback without restarting the old program</h2>
    <p>Retain the original configuration and enough account access to inspect historical branch decisions through the agreed handover period. If the replacement fails, pause its entry and dispatch queues first, reconcile sends and purchases since cutover, then resume only people still eligible in the retained system. Re-enabling every Drip workflow would replay already completed steps. Archive the mapping, event ledger and unresolved people with the receiving operator. The <a href="/apps/drip">Drip evaluation</a> covers initial account fit; this replacement decision turns on whether the reconstructed journey is maintainable.</p>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
